n Stewart\nLimited preview - 2017\n\n### Bibliographic information\n\nTitle  | Infinity: A Very Short Introduction\nVery short introductions\nAuthor  | Ian Stewart\nPublisher  | Oxford University Press, 2017\nISBN  | 0191816574, 9780191816574\n   |  \nExport Citation  | BiBTeX EndNote RefMan--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCopyright Page | Infinity: A Very Short Introduction | Oxford Academic (https://academic.oup.com/book/972/chapter-abstract/137833526)\nciteturn357search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9.6 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; \n\nInfinity: A Very Short Introduction\n\nIan Stewart\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1093/actrade/9780198755234.001.0001\n\nPublished:\n\n23 March 2017\n\nOnline ISBN:\n\n9780191816574\n\nPrint ISBN:\n\n9780198755234\n\n[Input: Search in this book] Search in this book\n\nFront Matter\n\n# Copyright Page\n\nGet access\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1093/actrade/9780198755234.002.0004\n\n  * Published:\n\n## Extract\n\nImage: graphic\n\n> Great Clarendon Street, Oxford, OX2 6DP,\n> \n> United Kingdom\n> \n> Oxford University Press is a department of the University of Oxford.\n> \n> It furthers the University’s objective of excellence in research, scholarship,\n> \n> and education by publishing worldwide. Oxford is a registered trade mark of\n> \n> Oxford University Press in the UK and in certain other countries\n> \n> © Joat enterprises 2017\n> \n> The moral rights of the author have been asserted\n> \n> First edition published in 2017\n> \n> Impression: 1\n> \n> All rights reserved. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTIME OF THE MAGICIANS | Kirkus Reviews (https://www.kirkusreviews.com/book-reviews/wolfram-eilenberger/time-of-the-magicians/)\nciteturn357search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.2 years ago; Crawled: last month; \nAbly translated by Whiteside, Eilenberger’s book is the kind of limpid presentation of Continental philosophical expression rare in books about the subject. It’s an achievement that has already won plaudits and prizes abroad. That’s no doubt due to the author’s own professional standing as a philosopher, but it also owes much to his approach: a multilayered exploration of the lives and thoughts of four very different thinkers at a time when Western and Central Europe struggled to emerge from war and economic crisis before slipping into the horrors of Nazism. The imposing Ludwig Wittgenstein, the hapless Walter Benjamin, the always troubling Martin Heidegger, and the steady, placid Ernst Cassirer emerge from Eilenberger’s portrait as formidable minds attached to flawed personalities whose sometimes barely comprehensible formulations nevertheless transformed the way human understanding is now seen by philosophers. The book’s special value lies in greatly advancing accessibility to these men’s works and thought. So clear and sometimes jaunty is Eilenberger that no reader will miss out from understanding the narrative. One can complain only that he, too, rarely makes known his own views. Otherwise, his lucid presentation of his characters’ often hard-to-comprehend thinking and the muddy language in which they expressed it make this book invaluable for anyone seeking to learn about these extraordinary figures.\n\nAn exemplary work of scholarship that is comprehensible to everyone. (16-page b/w photo insert)\n\nPub Date: Aug. 18, 2020\n\nISBN: 978-0-525-55966-5\n\nPage Count: 432\n\nPublisher: Penguin Press\n\nReview Posted Online: April 8, 2020\n\nKirkus Reviews Issue: May 1, 2020"
